\title{tulpaRatio: Bayesian Hierarchical Models for Ratios, Rates, and Proportions}
\description{
Bayesian hierarchical models for ratios, rates and proportions, in which the numerator and the denominator are two processes with linked linear predictors and the ratio is derived from their joint posterior. The denominator is either a second response or a known exposure. Both predictors take shared or process-specific random effects, latent factors, intrinsic and proper conditional autoregressive fields (Besag, York and Mollie (1991) \doi{10.1007/BF00116466}), the scaled Besag-York-Mollie field of Riebler et al. (2016) \doi{10.1177/0962280216660421}, nearest-neighbour Gaussian processes (Datta et al. (2016) \doi{10.1080/01621459.2015.1044091}), temporal random walks and autoregressions, Knorr-Held space-time interactions, and zero-inflated or hurdle numerators. The default sampler is the No-U-Turn sampler with hand-coded gradients; elliptical slice sampling, Polya-Gamma Gibbs sampling (Polson, Scott and Windle (2013) \doi{10.1080/01621459.2013.829001}) and a nested Laplace approximation fit the subsets of models they apply to, and every fit reports the tier of guarantee its inference carries.

An opinionated framework for Bayesian hierarchical modelling of ratios,
rates, and proportions. The core premise is non-negotiable:

\strong{Ratios are not data. They are derived quantities.}

All inference is performed on the latent processes generating the numerator
and the denominator, never on their quotient. Ratios, rates, and proportions
are computed post hoc with full uncertainty propagation.
}
